    "turn_idx": 125,
    "turn_text": "So just transition that to federal law that was passed based upon then known science versus today's known science and also handling and exposure and the like. Everyone would argue we have to update this. So we've got a bill called Chemicals and Commerce Act. I think the word was well taken. In this studio, the chemical manufacturer industry would say 95% of everything here was touched by chemical manufacturers. Now the question is, is that mean that this all toxic and we're all going to eventually die because of our contact? I sure should not. Of course not, but should we know what we have, and should we prioritize that, and should we then use sound science to determine if it's safe, let people know it's safe, if it needs to be specially handled, make sure that people know it's specially handled, it's highly toxic. Can we eventually move to another chemical to replace it? That's kind of the basis of the legislation, very excited about it, and working closely with some Democrats on the other side. There is a bipartisan bill in the Senate. It was Lautenberg before he passed, and it was Vitter, David Vitter, and now it's Vitter and Udall. That kind of started the ball. It is. Tom Udall from New Mexico. And that started the ball moving forward that there is some middle ground on this. We've taken that and done a little more due diligence and cleaned up the language, but we need a big bipartisan vote on the floor. to wake up Senator Barbara Boxer that we do have a bipartisan bill and that's what we're",
